The initial implementations and related tests/fixtures for parts of the parser
layer were adapted from this project. The affected area includes shared parser
aggregation patterns and source readers for Kimi Code, Claude Code, Codex,
OpenCode, Gemini CLI, Antigravity, GitHub Copilot CLI, Cursor, and Roo Code,
as well as later Pi Coding Agent, ZCode, and WorkBuddy compatibility work,
including supporting SQLite utilities where applicable. Subsequent adaptations
include Grok CLI, Trae CLI, MiniMax Code, Qoder/Qoder CN and DeepSeek Harness
(DSH, session formats V0–V4) readers and corresponding test fixtures.

The OpenCode reader's 2.x store compatibility (sqlite_master table detection,
session_message projection reads with session/session_v2 directory joins,
legacy-priority deduplication for migrated stores, and explicit warnings for
unrecognized table layouts) is adapted from upstream commit
f1dd16688826e8005b7d71a14a1b4bacc3e74cfd (2026-09-26) and re-expressed in this
repository's separate SQLite/JSON reader structure and five-field
mutually-exclusive token contract. That commit's write-cache pricing change has
no equivalent here, because this project already counts tokens.cache.write in
its own cacheWriteInputTokens bucket.

The MiniMax Code reader's multi-store data-root coverage (the CLI's own
MINIMAX_DATA_DIR / MAVIS_DATA_DIR relocation variables, per-profile
~/.minimax-<profile> homes, the pre-npm ~/.minimax-code and pre-rename ~/.mavis
stores, physical-path deduplication for compat links, cross-store folding of
byte-identical ledger rows that keeps duplicates inside a single store, and
degraded extra-store warnings instead of blanking the live store, plus the
matching background-service environment whitelist entries) is adapted from
upstream commit af609278e0e27f73910b1481964f423ef3561546 (2026-09-28) and
re-expressed in this repository's parser-level root resolver, five-field
mutually-exclusive token contract with request counts, English warning text,
and the registry roots() gating that decides whether a source is installed.

The subscription-limit contract's optional machine-readable emptyReason
vocabulary for empty quota results (limitReached / noWindow / notEntitled /
sessionWithoutPlanLimits, with unknown values rejected instead of passed
through) and the OpenCode Go HTTP 403 → notEntitled mapping for a valid API
key without an active Go subscription are adapted from upstream commit
1287a8a3a42da0a5b74222778fbd94781f546e5b (2026-09-27) and re-expressed in this
repository's assertProviderContract browser allowlist instead of a dedicated
schema module. This repository keeps its single contract revision and does not
introduce a schemaVersion mechanism; only the API-key path maps 403, while the
Cookie workspace path keeps its existing failure semantics.

The OpenCode Go quota path's read-only local credential discovery — selecting
only the opencode-go integration key from OpenCode's own credential table
through the shared read-only SQLite helper, falling back to the 1.x auth.json
`opencode.key` entry when the table holds no Go row or cannot be read, and
reporting a concrete store failure only when the fallback has no key either —
is adapted from upstream commits
166f735084a0ed55b60a1db5e8345ce38d9f6651 (2026-09-22) and
e21bd2d6fa6058bd6f319d193e06f6dc9c7b2d50 (2026-09-26), and re-expressed in
this repository's limits provider: local discovery is an explicit connection
mode with a credential-scoped, hashed history identity. Manually configured
API Key / Cookie + Workspace accounts never fall back to another local login.
The OpenCode store stays
strictly read-only, and the key never reaches the result, history, logs, or
cache. Upstream's quota registry entry, schema list, disposable cache-scope
hashing, and VIBE_USAGE_OPENCODE_DIRS override have no equivalent here; this
repository reuses its existing KBU_USAGE_OPENCODE_DIR data-root override and
honors its XDG_DATA_HOME resolution instead. Upstream commit
6530c6d5f7f44c710cc324db989472dbe7a8f3cd (2026-09-27), which builds the data
root test expectations with node:path's join and the platform delimiter so
they hold on Windows, is reflected in the corresponding tests here.

The Kimi Code quota path's support for the current CLI 2.x generation —
probing the official OAuth credential across `$KIMI_SHARE_DIR`, then
`$KIMI_CODE_HOME` / `~/.kimi-code`, then the legacy `~/.kimi` and keeping the
first existing login (naming the 2.x path when no login exists), together with
parsing the 2.x keyed `usages` response (limit_5h / limit_7d /
limit_month_total / limit_month_code entries with used_ratio and reset
