CodexBar provider integrations
------------------------------

Project:    CodexBar
Source:     https://github.com/steipete/CodexBar
License:    MIT
Copyright:  Copyright (c) 2026 Peter Steinberger
Audited snapshot: 211781be7b815cbd93d676a83ab6e7fbb22c9ded
Additional Coding Plan review: f9bc62c344b3ba5fa758632c94a5c43af8fae90e (2026-09-12)

Provider-specific endpoint knowledge, request shapes, response mapping, local
credential discovery, and related test cases in the subscription-limit layer
were adapted or reimplemented from CodexBar's provider integrations. This
particularly applies to Codex, Claude, Cursor, GitHub Copilot, OpenCode Go,
Kiro, Qoder, Warp, Antigravity, DeepSeek, and JetBrains AI where the
corresponding source exists upstream.

The October 2026 correctness adaptations include Kimi matching-cycle counter /
ratio reconciliation (8ab81e2ebd592589f2c4c47fccd689a00dc19e31), JetBrains monthly
versus purchased-credit balance selection (ea2babcd31e7228fba3aafcaddf55f1e8271cadf),
bounded same-installation idea.log quota parsing (39ba7c649da14dd5a6d9973febb3012797c1df6e),
and Antigravity unsupported consumer OAuth client rejection before placeholder
quota publication (9699239f0ddc16777c84e5cc6116e526e9a4f71a). Codex request identity,
ownership, and mirror-accounting safeguards were informed by
a555a82bad569e801e1628a43ceb507b0764b337. These are re-expressed in JavaScript
with sanitized fixture tests and the existing provider / collector contracts;
no account-promotion, automatic sign-in, or provider app management is included.

The second October 2026 batch adapts WorkBuddy resource-summary/package-list
request shapes from Resources/Plugins/workbuddy.ts; Claude reset inventory,
promotional cloud credits, and verified subscription metadata from the Claude
provider; and measured Codex turn timing rules from CostUsageScanner's turn
performance implementation. WorkBuddy uses an explicit Cookie/User-Agent tuple
instead of upstream browser import. Claude metadata verifies the exact account
and organization without importing cookies. Codex performance stays local-only,
requires complete owned durable usage, and never enters community uploads.

The later GLM / Z.ai, MiniMax Coding / Token Plan, and Alibaba Coding Plan
integrations adapt endpoint/request shapes and sanitized quota fixtures from
the corresponding CodexBar providers. They retain separate quota units, omit
unverifiable capacity, isolate region-specific credentials/history, and do not
copy cross-region credential retries or custom endpoint overrides. Alibaba's
new personal/team Token Plan and GLM team scopes are not included.

The Antigravity OAuth quota path preferring the grouped model-family summary
endpoint (v1internal:retrieveUserQuotaSummary) over the per-model fallback
chain, with weekly-only Starter free-tier pools reported at an explicit weekly
cadence, is adapted from upstream commit
62acfd8ead86a3ae443ec2d76c30c93ec72e09b4 (2026-09-28) and re-expressed in this
repository's single JavaScript provider module. The grouped summary parser
itself already existed here for the local loopback path, so only the endpoint
preference, its logged-out error semantics, and the fallback order were added.

The Codex OAuth quota path's bounded auth.json reread — retrying the local
credential read twice at 50 ms intervals when the file is missing, only
partially published, or its access token is expired or about to expire, before
reporting the login as missing or stale, without ever redeeming the CLI's own
refresh token — together with the plan-change invalidation that stops an old
subscription's quota/reset evidence from being presented as the new plan's
current evidence, is adapted from upstream commit
9013fd81b218dc211f4230188b3291d7bfa75eed (2026-09-28) and re-expressed in this
repository's JavaScript credential loader and Codex provider. Upstream
invalidates reset backfill and weekly reset confirmation baselines at
publication time; the equivalent here is an optional sanitized plan identity
on subscription-history observations (history schema v2) plus segmentation of
pooled observation evidence by the current plan in the dashboard insights,
where records without a plan stay unknown-plan evidence. The settings
detection path keeps its single read, matching upstream availability probing.

The Kimi monthly-membership binding cap — projecting shorter quota windows as
blocked while a longer monthly pool is observed exhausted, carrying the
blocking pool's reset horizon without promising an earlier recovery time, and
clearing pace for the blocked display — together with the Z.ai / GLM explicit
"Coding Plan usage unavailable" annotation when a quota response contains
unsupported limit shapes or no convertible quota, is adapted from upstream
commit e9b2823d6fd0e883d392f23b42038757f67b39ea (2026-09-28). Upstream applies
the projection while building its menu-card model and replaces the displayed
percentage there; the equivalent here keeps every raw provider window and
history observation untouched and expresses the block as a derived dashboard
display marker plus a provider notice, so no projected number is persisted as
a provider observation and no quota amount is invented.

The Kimi stale-CLI-credential recovery copy — directing an expired or rejected Kimi Code
CLI login to run `kimi` to renew, noting that the next query rereads the credential file so
restarting this tool is unnecessary, naming the unattended `KIMI_AUTH_TOKEN`
environment-variable/keychain alternative, and recording that Kimi rotates refresh tokens
so an in-memory-only refresh would invalidate the CLI's saved token while writing back can
race with the CLI's own renewal — is adapted from upstream commit
6dcac3df4b1b8f077ab4d3704f711797ec364e31 (2026-09-28) and re-expressed in this
repository's provider error copy and a new Kimi Code login document. Upstream keeps
CLI-owned credentials strictly read-only; this repository deliberately keeps its existing
redeem-and-write-back renewal with its own process lock, reread under the lock,
400-triggered reread, and atomic rename, so upstream's read-only stance and its
KIMI_CODE_API_KEY setting have no equivalent here.

The Cursor forbidden-request back-off — classifying a geo-blocked HTTP 403 on
quota fetches as its own error category instead of an expired login, cooling
the affected provider down for at least six hours on a provider-and-account
scope so automatic refreshes stop re-requesting a region-blocked endpoint on
every poll, and lifting the cooldown immediately on manual refresh, account or
settings changes, and cache clears — is adapted from upstream commit
1c0f70e3618d3c0c3c2e0e3648f0166e0530414d (2026-09-23) and re-expressed in this
repository's Cursor-specific service error classifier and in-memory cooldown
map. Other providers retain their authentication recovery, including MiniMax's
same-region legacy endpoint retry on HTTP 403. The cooldown state exists only inside the running process, never
reaches the payload, history, exports, or community sync, and upstream's
per-provider token-publication scope signatures have no equivalent here because
settings saves already clear this repository's whole limit cache.

The implementation here translates those provider facts into JavaScript and a
different normalized quota contract, then adds a provider allowlist, loopback-
only credential boundary, macOS Keychain handling, redacted browser responses,
isolated refresh failures, and the Kimi Builders setup UI. No CodexBar binary is
distributed in this package.


